Kanban method: Implement this simple card system and improve productivity
If you remember when the subject of JIT (Just-In Time) or Toyota method, it sure will ring a bell the Kanban concept. Basically it is an information method capable of providing greater control to the manufacturing processes, making the productivity of the factory improve.
It's based on three basic points in order to carry out this process control:
Easy reading of indications: With a single glance, you can already have an idea of what is happening. That's thanks to the colored cards to distinguish the types of jobs, priority, labels, deadlines, times, etc.
As you can see, an easy method to implement, use, and update for companies, but with great benefits for managing tasks in a very visual way.
A Kanban board is a very practical tool for visually mapping or displaying tasks or workflow. It can be a panel or digital or physical whiteboard, divided into columns and rows.
Columns: in them you can see the phase of the process. For example, the columns can be:
In Progress - Tasks that are already in progress, are in progress but have not finished.
Others: there could be others if necessary, and even that each of the above is subdivided into several sections. For example, Requested could have a Ready To Start column, or those that are already ready to start, while those that are pending, but cannot be done yet, would be separated in another column.
Cards: each task that needs to be entered in the workflow will have its card in the corresponding column and row. Our Story
They could improve the way projects were developed and how final products or services were delivered. The same happened with many other sectors of the industry, and not only the motor industry, which have seen improvements thanks to adopting these new models.
The Kanban methodology is based on a series of basic principles What are they:
Efficiency: not only repair costs are reduced, waste is also reduced and production is focused on doing what is right and necessary (YAGNI principle), ensuring that everything necessary has the right attention.
Feedback: Kanban is not simply implemented, it must also update and improve the processes to achieve the objectives set by the company.
The Kanban method has obvious benefits. But if you need corroborating data, there is an experiment by BBC Worldwide London that incorporated this method, seeing its delivery time reduced by 37% and consistency in deliveries improved by 47%.
Performance improvement- Adjustment of workflow and flexibility allows productivity to be improved.
Organization- Digital or physical Kanban dashboards enable better real-time organization for work.
A Kanban method not only involves putting a dashboard in the company physical or digital in which to paste post-it. The implementation effort is somewhat more complex than that.
Constant and flexible flow: you must have control over the work flow and follow up to adapt to the needs, without interrupting this flow. The system must allow enough flexibility to change the priority in real time if necessary.
