These 2 families of devices are of the RISC type (reduced instruction set). This means that its set of instructions is very reduced in the order of 30 to 200 instructions that will be executed, except for some of them, in the order of 1 machine cycle.
In this type of architecture, the data memory is separated from the program memory, so they are connected to the CPU through completely independent separate buses that can be of different widths. Due to this, the size of the instructions is not related to the size of the data and therefore can be optimized so that any instruction occupies a single position in program memory.
Both the microcontrollers from the microchip firm and the microcontrollers from the ATMEL firm are of Harvard architecture.
All positions in RAM function as source and / or destination registers for math operations and other functions.
AVR Main Features:
All the instructions except some jump instructions occupy 1 machine cycle which is equal to 1 or 2 clock cycles.
In the previous section we saw that its main characteristics were a microcontroller as well as a quick check of its architecture.
For this purpose, we have chosen the C language because it has great advantages, among which we can highlight:
It offers great flexibility when programming, because the user can take a unique style, based on the structures of the language.
Many of the tools used to develop applications using microcontrollers can be located by doing a quick search on the web. To facilitate the task of this search for the reader, we list the most used below ...
For both the PIC and AVR microcontrollers there are many tools that we can use either paid or free distribution. For the development of our course we will essentially need 3:
As previously mentioned, the AVR family devices were created with the idea of executing compiled C code efficiently.
There are multiple development platforms for both the ATMEL and MICROCHIP family devices.
This software may or may not act in conjunction with AVRGCC, allowing the user to order their projects, and also give the possibility of correcting, simulating and recording the developed program on the microcontroller.
For the PIC family of microcontrollers there is the MPLAB. This programming platform, like AVR Studio, offers us the possibility of having our projects organized, as well as simulating, correcting errors and recording the program being developed on the device.
As we also write our programs, compilers generate executable code analogously to an executable in Windows or Linux. It has a ".hex" extension (see ".hex" format).
In the market there are many tools that fulfill this function, but the ones most used by amateur and professional developers (due to the characteristics they have) are the following:
PLEASE NOTE: a research I recently conducted shows that this tool is used to record PIC and AVR microcontrollers, without making any modifications to the hardware and firmware of the programmer. Only with a small application can we add this functionality, it will be developed later.
This device produced by the ATMEL company in conjunction with the AVR Studio suite will be used to record the AVR microcontrollers. As with the PICKIT 2 there are many clones in the network, therefore later I will also teach you how to make one ...
Previously we saw the tools that were needed to be able to develop applications with PIC and AVR microcontrollers. In this section we will see how both types of devices are programmed.
We reduce development time, since you do not have to remove the device from the motherboard where it is, you just have to connect the programmer to its ICSP socket.
We can correct errors and test programs directly on the application card.
(The other method is called low voltage or LVP). Using this method, 5 volts must be entered through the RB3 / PGM leg depending on whether it is PIC16F or PIC18F (See programming manual for each family of devices).
The setup time (time that the data must be before the falling edge arrives) and the hold time (the one that must be after the falling edge occurs) are 100ns.
In total, 16 falling edges are needed to send the data, and 6 falling edges for the commands. 18 falling edges are used for the PICs of line 18F.
The minimum time between sending a command and reading or writing data must be 1 microsecond.
At this level are the different commands of the recording service, there are a variety of different commands depending on the family of devices being used. These commands are used to write data to program memory, read data from data memory, enter programming mode.
Unlike PIC microcontrollers, AVR family devices differ by not needing high voltage for the recording process but using the same supply voltage for all their operations.
In total this system uses 4 cables to establish communication through SPI.
The first commands are sent after a period of 20ms.
The first command sent is the "enable programming mode" command
The read / write cycles of the program memory, data memory and fuses are started.
