Articles, videos, links and resources on the different types of kites and their uses.
The truth is that flying kites was a meditation exercise for the Chinese. But, in addition to these recreational or religious uses, kites were used -as they would also do in Polynesia years later- as fishing gear, tying a hook with a bait to a kite that was later released from a boat, being careful to place the device at a sufficient distance from the shadow cast by the boat to deceive the fish.
The kite extends from China throughout its area of influence; thus, it appears in Southeast Asian countries such as Korea, the Malay Archipelago, Indonesia and Burma, and also in Japan and India. It is unknown when this expansion took place, but if it is likely to take place in a short space of time, as the kite soon begins to spread throughout Polynesia, becoming known as far east as Easter Island.
After the Second World War, the American engineer Francis Rogallo developed a kite that opened the way to the new shape known as semi-flexible, characterized by acquiring its shape thanks to the combination of the action of the wind and a system of six flanges. NASA became interested in Rogallo's idea, which he planned to use as a parachute in the reentry of space capsules.
Nowadays the appearance of the two, three and four-wire stunt kites and the traction kites have made it popular as a sport and fun.
