I've been testing the recipe for the Galalith or milk plastic and the results have been a bit disappointing.
I have heated the milk and before boiling I have put it in a glass, I have put food coloring in some cases and then the vinegar. Lumps form almost instantly, flocs from a paste that is casein.
On the other hand, in pieces where I have applied some pressure, the results have been much better.
A rigid and light plastic remains. After a week it still exudes "oil" but although it still gives a feeling of fragility, something could already be done with this quality.
For future experiments
Casein is insoluble in water and acid, although contact with them or alkalis can cause cracking. It is odorless, biodegradable, non-allergenic, antistatic, and virtually non-flammable (it burns slowly and brilliantly in air, but extinguishes upon removal of the source of the flame.
