The water rocket It is a simple homemade rocket based on the principle of action and reaction. Its operation consists solely of putting air into a plastic bottle with water and when the pressure becomes very great the rocket will be propelled by a jet of pressurized water.
In principle this was going to be a post, but I have found so much information about the Water Rockets o Water rockets I've decided to turn it into a section. In this way we can gather much more information.
For the one who has never heard of a water rocket, is a plastic bottle, partially filled with water, into which pressurized air is introduced and then let it escape through an outlet hole and propel the bottle.
With MRP you can improve the profitability of a business without having to sell more quantity of products or services. This may seem counterintuitive, but it is not.
MRP stands for materials planning, or Material Requirements Planning. A process in which the company focuses on planning the materials necessary to improve the production of its product or service.
All objectives of the MRP they are quite clear, and what is intended is:
Those were the first germs of what is now MRP, although it was still very immature and could not be considered a complete MRP method. But after the war, when the factories had to adapt again to production for civilian use, with what they learned during the conflict, inventory control, production and logistics could be improved.
With the appearance of the first computers, the software MRP it would further help the deployment in more companies and improve their efficiency. In this way, in the 70s the concept would appear as we know it today.
With the advent of software, everything is much easier, and it is possible to automatically calculate the quantities of product to be produced and the exact quantities of material necessary for manufacturing. But for this, the demand must be carefully analyzed.
MRP I systems date back to the 60-70s, and do not cover some aspects of the most modern production. That's why it arose an evolution known as MRP II in the 80s.
In these programs there are entries that go through the master production schedule, the list of components or materials needed, and the current inventory status. With this, the software will process the information to offer some results in the form of outputs, such as inventory forecast, build-to-order schedule, and other reports.
Another concept that you should know is the BOM (Bill of Materials), that is, the list of materials necessary for the production of the final product.
As I mentioned in the previous section, some of these programs are really ERP tools, and it is that on many occasions both concepts can be confused. In fact, ERP does some of the functions of MRP, so a lot of existing software can do both in a single suite.
The MRP arises from the experience of a company, while the ERP needs to adapt the production of a company so that it can be applied.
MRP II also allows simulation to predict future demand, something that ERP does not do.
Like any method, MRP has its advantages and disadvantages. Something important that needs to be evaluated before implementing the system in an industry.
Inventory reduction with savings of up to 40% on investments.
Lower sale prices mean more satisfied customers.
It allows to provide flexibility to adapt the model and adapt to the necessary times.
HR disadvantages they are reduced if the implementation is done with a commitment to comply with the MRP model and there are no mistakes (and there usually are, since great precision is needed to work properly). In other words, the drawbacks often occur when MRP is used improperly and it is forgotten that MRP is just a software tool and does not involve making decisions.
