Although their origin is uncertain, comets are supposed to have been born in China more than 2.500 years ago. There are several legends about its origin: a bamboo hat of a farmer carried by the wind, the sail of a ship or perhaps the work of the philosopher Mo Ti, who built a kite in the shape of a bird that was flying for three days like the birds
The Chinese also found military applications to this object, which could be from signaling on the battlefield, to measuring the distance to a besieged camp and, even, in some chronicles it is said that there were men who flew in these objects over cities besieged.
The inventions of William A. Eddy (1894) and L. Hargrave (XNUMX) date from this period, which led to a revolution in their use in the study of the atmosphere. The first made a conventional kite with the stringer arched to form a dihedral angle, which allowed it to fly without a tail; the second's kite was a three-dimensional structure consisting of two boxes connected to each other with open ends.
It is a prismatic kite with a triangular base, and for its construction we will use straws for the structure and tissue paper.
