I say, “Away with such a false gospel!
It has damned millions of souls, crippled our churches, and has brought spiritual ruin to our nation!
Second, this parable was given by Christ to show how lost sinners, dead in trespasses and sins, are saved! I once knew a man who left his wife and ran off with another woman.
He stared at me with a wild look in his eyes, like he had been caught!
Jesus gave three parables in the fifteenth chapter of Luke to answer the Pharisees. They had complained that he ate with sinners (Luke 15:2).
The weight of all these commentators shows clearly that the Prodigal was a lost man, and the parable shows how he was converted. That is the view given by mainstream scholars throughout the ages – until the “decisionism” of our time made conversion “fuzzy” and unclear!
Third, this parable shows what must happen to you in a real conversion.
1.Admit to yourself that your heart is truly selfish and wants to be as far away from God as possible. We have known people who came to the inquiry room and said they wanted to be saved who were, at the very same time, planning to leave the church!
The way you are now, you have no inner peace!
When you find Christ it will be well worth any amount of effort, any amount of “striving.” Jesus said, “Come unto me…and I will give you rest” (Matthew 11:28). “The blood of Jesus Christ his Son cleanseth us from all sin” (I John 1:7).
I’ve wandered far away from God,
The paths of sin too long I’ve trod,
I’ve wasted many precious years,
I now repent with bitter tears,
Coming home, coming home,
First, the revelation of the apostasy.
Today there is a falling away from what the Bible teaches about the literal Blood atonement. Preachers may say they believe it, but I don’t know any major preacher who is proclaiming the Blood of Christ as Spurgeon and Dr. Lloyd-Jones did.
“Let no man deceive you by any means: for that day shall not come, except there come a falling away first” – hē apostasia – the apostasy (II Thessalonians 2:3).
But my long-time pastor at the Chinese church, Dr. Timothy Lin, saw something else here. He said, “Satan knows more about prophecy than many Christians.” He said that the Devil knows right now that he only has a short time before he is bound for a thousand years in “the bottomless pit” (Revelation 20:1-2; p. 1349).
Don’t forget that the Devil goes to church! Yes, Satan goes to church every Sunday!
I could feel my flesh crawl and my hair stand on end – when I read the account of Charles G. Finney’s (1792-1875) so-called conversion, and so-called Spirit-filling!
A friend of mine, with an earned doctorate in theology, said, “There’s a darkness about Finney. He was a tormented man.” Then that preacher said, “Have you ever seen a photograph of his eyes?
Very few church members are saved today. They have 3 or 4-year-olds say the words of a so-called “sinner’s prayer” and then they baptize them!
Christ saves us from the Apostasy! Come out of the dying culture of America, and its dying churches!
On Christ, the solid Rock, I stand,
Tonight, there is no safe place in this world except in Jesus!
“Let no man deceive you by any means: for that day shall not come, except there come a falling away first” (II Thessalonians 2:3).
First, there are different kinds of loneliness.
The campus doesn't seem to draw people together into permanent relationships. Young people attending a university or college often feel like the whole world is passing them by, and no one understands them or cares about them.
I believe that loneliness is one of the main reasons so many young people commit suicide today. Did you know that suicide is the third greatest cause of death among young people between the ages of 15 and 24 years old?
Second, you can contribute to your own loneliness.
That's what Cain found out. God said to him,
The person who sins and runs away from the church will become a wandering, lonely person, just like Cain!
"Woe unto them! for they have gone in the way of Cain"
That's the reason we say: Why be lonely?
That's why God gave Adam a wife named Eve. And Adam's wife is a type of the church according to the Scofield Reference Bible.
That means that Adam's wife is an illustration or picture of the local church. God said,
We are here to help you overcome loneliness!
Come home to church!
But, thirdly, you have a deeper loneliness.
You see, coming into this church, and coming here several times every week will cure your loneliness for friends. But what about your loneliness for God?
"Your iniquities have separated between you and your God, and your sins have hid his face from you…" (Isaiah 59:2). There is a separation between you and God caused by your sin.
"Wherefore come out from among them, and be ye separate, saith the Lord, and touch not the unclean thing; and I will receive you, And will be a Father unto you, and ye shall be my sons and daughters, saith the Lord Almighty" (II Corinthians 6:17-18). The doctrine of separation is one of the most important teachings in the Bible.
"Be ye not unequally yoked together with unbelievers: for what fellowship hath righteousness with unrighteousness? and what communion hath light with darkness?"
Focus on those words, "I have chosen you out of the world." Read those words out loud, "I have chosen you out of the world."
"I have manifested thy name unto the men which thou gavest me out of the world …" You may be seated.
Thus, we see that we are called out of the world and into the local church.
Biblical separation takes you out of fellowship with the world and into the fellowship of the local church. You leave the world behind, and make a whole new set of friends in the church.
Third, separation is a matter of the heart.
"Ye adulterers and adulteresses, know ye not that the friendship of the world is enmity with God? whosoever therefore will be a friend of the world is the enemy of God" (James 4:4).
"Whosoever therefore will be a friend of the world is the enemy of God." You may be seated.
You are called out of the world to Christ. The person who refuses to stop looking to the friendship and comfort of the world will not look to Christ.
"Wherefore come out from among them, and be ye separate…and I will receive you, And will be a Father unto you…" (II Corinthians 6:17-18).
Will you come out of the world, and trust Jesus, the Son of God?
